 "trivia": "The dish is usually described as a French colonial inheritance — a beef stew arriving with beef-eating colonists and being taken up locally — and that account is half true and misses the more interesting half. What arrived was the animal at scale, not the technique: slow-simmered spiced meat in liquid is old in the region and the kho family is entirely local. What the encounter actually produced was the eating position. Bò Kho is served with a baguette, in the morning, on the street, and every one of those three is a hybrid of the two kitchens rather than a borrowing from either. The stew is Vietnamese, the bread is French, and the hour is nobody's — Paris would not eat beef stew at seven and neither would Hanoi have eaten it with a loaf a century earlier.",
